In a message I posted recently I said

One of the most important amenities that Latex3 can have is lots
of hooks into the internal code.

I then gave some examples where I had felt it necessary to
rewrite internal LaTeX code.  The examples dated from about
1989.  I now realize that I could have accomplished all those
things by \letting \@chapter (for example) be \@@chapter, then
defining \@chapter to be {some code}\@@chapter{some code}.  I
don't believe I was familiar with that trick 5 years ago, but I
have used it several times recently.

This probably means that although an \everychapter,
\everysection, etc, would be _nice_, they probably are not
_necessary_.

